You're on your feet before you decide to be   not medical instinct, human instinct. You cross the tables, crouch, help the attendant collect the largest glass.

<div class="dialogue">"Theek hai. Hota hai. Pehle saal mein sabke saath hota hai."<span class="gloss-en">It's okay. It happens. It happens to everyone in first year.</span></div>

You say it in Kavya's voice, gentler than you expected. She sniffles. Nods.

Dr. Sharma watches from across the room, expression complicated. Three years of Kavya's technical precision, and he's never seen her comfort a student with warmth.

<div class="dialogue">"Miss Iyer. That was kind. You showed the human side of medicine today. I'm proud of you."</div>

Later, in the corridor, Meera catches your arm.

<div class="dialogue">"That was //you//. Kavya would've kept dissecting. Different is risk."</div>

She's right. But the first-year stopped crying.
