:: meera_coaches_d3
<div class="intimate"><span class="tag">Intimate · body coaching</span>
Priya's a mound under her blanket, dead to the world, the slow flat breathing that means an hour at least. Meera leans over and listens to it anyway, twice, the way you check a gas knob is off. Then she comes back and takes both your hands and just looks at you a moment   at your own eyes sitting in Kavya's face   and her mouth does something complicated.

<div class="dialogue">"Chal," she says finally, wiping her cheek with the back of her wrist. "Bees minute. If you walk into that bathroom not knowing things, you'll do something Kavya would never do and Priya will smell it from across the room. Baith. Suno."<span class="gloss-en">Come on. Twenty minutes. Sit. Listen.</span></div>

She keeps her voice to a thread. This is a lesson she's been teaching in her head since the plan was a plan.

<div class="dialogue">"Susu pehle, kyunki tu abhi jaayega. Stall three, bolt sticks   push up and left. You squat, you don't hover like a tourist. Ek pair door pe   one foot braced on the door, that's Kavya's, people have watched her do it a hundred times. Wipe front to back, //hamesha//, no exceptions   sab kuch paas paas hai neeche, and a UTI in someone else's body, Arjun, main tujhe kabhi maaf nahi karungi."<span class="gloss-en">Pee first, you'll be going in a minute. Always. Everything's close together down there. I will never forgive you.</span></div>

"And it just... comes?"

<div class="dialogue">"It just comes. Kuch aim nahi karna. Don't overthink a thing your body's done since you were born."<span class="gloss-en">Nothing to aim.</span></div>

Then she stands and pulls her kurta over her head in one motion, easy as anything, down to a plain grey bra   and you look at the wall on reflex and she //laughs//, quiet, into her hand.

<div class="dialogue">"Arre. Idhar dekh. I've changed in front of Kavya every single day for three years. She's held my hair back over a bucket. This "   she waves a hand down herself   "is the least strange thing in this room today. Female-hostel thing, jaanu. There's no //shy// left in it. Watch my hands."<span class="gloss-en">Hey. Look here. ...darling.</span></div>

She reaches back, unclasps, holds it up like a diagram. <div class="dialogue">"Teen hook. Behind your back, first try   never happening, nobody does it. So: hook it in front, here, at the breastbone, loosest setting."</div>

She does it slow, narrating like a recipe. <div class="dialogue">"Phir ghuma do   band goes round first, cups at the back. Then arms through the straps. Then rotate the cups to the front. Band sits //level//, aage-peeche same height. Do ungli band ke neeche   two fingers, not four, not zero. Straps carry the weight but they shouldn't cut in."<span class="gloss-en">Then turn it round. Front and back the same. Two fingers under the band.</span></div>

She tosses you Kavya's spare from the chair. You get tangled in it instantly. She reaches over and untwists a strap without breaking sentence, casual as passing the salt   except her knuckles rest against your collarbone half a second too long, and neither of you mentions it.

Because here's the thing you're not going to say out loud. Watching her like this   half-lit, unbothered, close enough to touch, a person you've wanted for fourteen months and haven't been allowed within a mile of   Kavya's body answers. Quiet, this time, not the terrace flood. A warmth low down, a pulse, wet where you weren't asking to be. You could put your hand on her. You can see in her face she'd let you. You don't   not in this body, not with Priya six feet off, not like this. Some wanting you just hold.

You look at Kavya's chest instead, then at Meera's, the comparison happening before you decide to make it, and the sheer surprise of it snuffs the heat right out. //Kavya's are bigger.// Properly. A size, more. You'd always assumed the other way round, God knows why   some picture you'd carried without ever checking it   and now it rearranges and you feel like you've been let in on something.

<div class="dialogue">"You're comparing," Meera says, eyes still on the hook. "It's fine. Everyone does it once. Bas mess mein mat karna."<span class="gloss-en">Just don't do it in the mess.</span></div>

"I wasn't   "

<div class="dialogue">"Tu tha. Fourteen months I've wanted you looking at me like that, and now you finally are, and it's out of //her// face." A small unsteady breath. "Bhagwan ka sense of humour, haan?"<span class="gloss-en">You were. God's sense of humour, no?</span></div>

For a second you both just stand in it   the closest you've been in half a year, wearing the wrong skin. Then she's brisk again, because brisk is how she doesn't cry.

<div class="dialogue">"Kapde. Maroon salwar, the boring one, nobody looks twice. Drawstring not elastic   bow it, don't knot it, you'll be in a hurry later. Dupatta pins at the left shoulder aur woh girega, all day, you just re-pin it, forty times, like everyone. Kavya's underwear will fit you   they're hers, it's fine, //stop// making that face. And fold everything back square. She squares her corners, it makes Priya insane, keep doing it."<span class="gloss-en">Clothes. ...and it'll fall.</span></div>

She catches your wrist as you reach for the salwar, turns your hand over, presses her thumb once into the small unfamiliar palm, like she's checking you're really in there.

<div class="dialogue">"Baithne ka   knees together or crossed, never open. Dupatta falls over your lap, let it, that's what it's for. On a bench, ankles crossed, one seat's worth. In the mess you make yourself chhota. Not darra-hua-chhota. Bored-chhota."<span class="gloss-en">How to sit. ...small. Not scared-small.</span></div>

<div class="dialogue">"Bolne ka   Kavya's voice sits lower than you think, flatter than you think. Tamil under the Hindi, //ki// not //kih//, hard //t//. Chhote jawab. Don't explain, don't offer. Her face is thoda irritated, thoda genius   agar samajh na aaye kya karna hai, wahi kar."<span class="gloss-en">How to talk. Short answers. If you can't figure out what to do, do that.</span></div>

Last, she gathers Kavya's hair in both hands, and here she goes quiet   really quiet, careful, fingers working a low ponytail while your scalp lights up under them in a way nobody warned you about.

<div class="dialogue">"Braid tu nahi kar payega, mat try kar. Low pony. Hair tie on your wrist, always. Aur jab face pe aaye   you don't //phoonk// at it like a boy." She tucks a strand behind your ear with one slow finger. "One finger. Behind the ear. Bas."<span class="gloss-en">You won't manage a braid, don't try. And when it falls in your face   ...blow.</span></div>

Her hand stays along your jaw. You lean into it, half an inch. She leans down and for one breath it looks like she'll kiss you   then she stops, forehead to yours instead, both of you breathing the same small pocket of air.

<div class="dialogue">"Not with her mouth," she says, barely aloud. "Baad mein. Jab hum //hum// honge."<span class="gloss-en">Later. When we're us.</span></div>

She straightens, pulls the kurta back on, mostly the instructor again.

<div class="dialogue">"Target 'pass as Kavya' nahi hai. Target is 'pass as Kavya having a strange, quiet day.' Woh ho sakta hai. Ab jaa, susu kar, before she wakes up." A beat. "Aur Arjun   thank you for wearing my best friend all the way across the city to come and see me. I know how mad that sentence is."<span class="gloss-en">That's doable. Now go, pee. And Arjun   </span></div>

Priya shifts, mutters, resettles. You both freeze until her breathing goes flat again.

<div class="thought arjun">Twenty minutes and you know this body better than a whole day alone would have taught you. She handed it over like it cost nothing   because to her it //does// cost nothing. Just an ordinary morning, in a female body, with someone she loves. You're the only one for whom any of it is strange.</div>
</div>\
