:: meera_close_d3
<div class="intimate"><span class="tag">The reason for all of it</span>
<<img "meera_close.png" "Room 304, the door bolted, the afternoon">>
Priya's gone until at least five   pathology group, then the library, complaining the whole way out. You bolt the door. Chain. And then you stop performing, for the first time since 5 AM: no Kavya-posture, no irritated face, no cover. Just you, in the wrong body, in a locked room with Meera.

She pulls you down onto her cot. You go. The mattress is thin and smells of her   Parachute oil, the sandalwood soap, sleep. She arranges you: your head on her shoulder, her arm around your back, and the geometry is upside-down from every version of this you've held in your head. You used to be the one she tucked under. Now you're small enough to tuck.

<div class="dialogue">"Chhe mahine," she says into Kavya's hair. "Six months. Pata hai aaj yeh do ghante teen logon ko kitne mehenge pade hain?"<span class="gloss-en">Do you know what these two hours are costing three people?</span></div>

"I know exactly what it's costing. I'd pay it again tomorrow. I //am// paying it again tomorrow."

You talk. Properly, the way you can't on a monitored phone. She tells you the real version of the last six months   not the brave WhatsApp version. Her father cancelled her Bharatanatyam arangetram two weeks before the date, in front of the guru. He reads her call logs on the family plan. She's been dancing alone in the bathroom with the tap running so the sound covers the ghungroos.

You tell her yours. The Redmi under the mattress. The nights coding until the grief blurred. The poem folder you'll deny exists.

<div class="dialogue">"I know about the folder. Kavya told me. Sixteen photos and a text file called //for_m//. Bewakoof."<span class="gloss-en">Idiot.</span></div>

